well after we've been reporting the  Prime Minister has been raped berated  for his use of language in the Commons  last night and after he said the best  way to honor the memory of the murdered  MP Joe Cox would be to get Briggs it  done joining me now from gamma soul and  West Yorkshire is Joe Cox's sister Kim  Ledbetter thank you very much indeed for  being with us what do you think of that  statement from the Prime Minister that  the best way to honor your sister's  memory is to get Briggs it done I  watched Parliament TV for four hours  last night and was mesmerised and  dumbstruck by the scenes that that I saw  before me and I think the Prime Minister  needs to think very carefully about the  language he uses I think everybody has  to think really carefully about the  language they use and and for me this is  not a party political matter this is  across the political spectrum it's  across the issue of brexit and I've been  saying this for two years now  unfortunately I'm not sure people are  listening  I think things did reach a bit of a head  last night and I'm hoping from some of  the conversations that are taking place  this morning that things will be  slightly karma going forward in the  coming days in the coming weeks but I'm  very clear that you know we all have a  responsibility to think about the  language we use the way we treat each  other and when we speak to each other on  a human level but I'm also very clear  about is the last thing I want the last  thing Joe would want is for her name to  be used in a way that silences debate  the robust discussions that are taking  place of a vitally important and we live  in a democracy or a very lucky that we  live in a democracy where we can have  these debates and discussions but I  think there is a tipping point where the  level of civility and the level of  respect you know becomes unacceptable  and I think unfortunately we witnessed  that in the chamber last night I  understand that but just on that  specific statement from the Prime  Minister about being the best way  Tornillo sisters merit memory being to  honor effectively what the seventeen  point four million voted for three and a  half years ago I mean that of itself is  not incendiary  he wasn't the one who introduced your  sister into the debate what what do you  think of that specific claim because  after all we know that Joe was an  alliance builder who wanted to work with  other parties and getting out of the  European Union wouldn't that be an  alliance a compromise well I think the  point he was trying to make if I'm if  I'm being fair is that the best way to  to bring people together is to find some  sort of solution to the problem that we  face I think and I hope that's what  that's what he meant and it's very  difficult for me to be objective about  it when Joe's name is used I think he he  probably got it wrong I think the  Homburg comment was was wrong and I  think to use Joe's name in that way was  wrong and what I hope is he has some  time to reflect on that and think about  it but I think the point he was making  and again I always try and be objective  I was trying to be fair about these  things is is that people want an end to  this chaos and I and I agree with him in  that respect you know it's very  difficult it was a very heated debate in  Parliament last night you know you could  feel the tension you could feel the  passion that was there and under those  circumstances people do make mistakes I  hope he has some time to reflect on on  what he said and and and realized that  possibly it wasn't the best thing to say  under those circumstances the  organizations you set up in memory of  Joe are about conciliation what's your  what's your thought on what we do about  Briggs it with quite clearly the country  still divided down the middle on this  question goodness me I wish I had the  answer to that I wouldn't be sat here  well dad we said we start somewhere far  more important than good muscle but and  I think we have to find a way forward  and ultimately that is going to come  down to compromise you know I think back  to our child and how Joe and I were  brought up and compromised as a big part  of that it's about listening to each  other it's about understanding different  perspectives not becoming entrenched in  our own positions I think that's the  only way we're going to find a way  forward and as we do that we have to do  that with respect for people who  disagree with us and that's what we do  through the work of the Joe Cox  foundation it's about understanding  difference it's about appreciating  difference  embracing it but doing so in a  respectful manner and ultimately coming  down to the fact that we do still have  things in common you know as joe said in  our famous speech it's very easy to  forget that and I think the humanity of  being in public life is what's what's  lost at the moment and we need to pull  pull that back you know it's a very  difficult job I sympathize with mp's  they've got a really really difficult  job to do  but I think also what we have to do is  is they have to take responsibility for  their actions as well and some of the  behavior we've seen across the political  debate I'm not a member of a political  party I don't have a political  allegiance I think you know everybody  whatever your colors are wherever you  are on break is it you have to think  about how you move forward from this and  listen to each other you know and I've  seen I've seen people you know calling  other people out who behaved as badly  themselves you know so I think that  that's that's my main point if I had  that the answer to Broca's it I'd be a  very rich woman wouldn't a Brava so  thank you very much indeed

